Batch Learning vs. Online Learning (Day 04) 

Note: These notes are compiled from the fourth lecture of the 100 Days of ML series by CampusX. They cover how machine learning models are trained and deployed in production environments, specifically focusing on Batch Learning (Offline Learning).

1. What is "Production" in Machine Learning?
In traditional software development, "production" simply means running your code on a server with an IP address so that your customers can access it over the internet.

In Machine Learning, production is a bit different:

You train your model.

You deploy the trained model onto a server.

The environment where the user interacts with the app is called the Client/User Environment, and the server running your ML model is called the Production Environment.

Depending on how these models are trained, updated, and executed in production, we divide machine learning into two major approaches:

Batch Learning (Offline Learning)

Online Learning (Incremental Learning)

2. What is Batch Learning (Offline Learning)?
Batch Learning is the traditional and most common way of training a machine learning model.

Definition: In this approach, you use your entire dataset all at once to train the model. There is no incremental training (meaning you don't train it in small pieces; you train it with the whole batch of data).

How it works: 1. Data scientists collect data on their local/offline machines.
2. The model is trained using this complete dataset.
3. Once the model is tested and verified, the trained model is uploaded/deployed to the production server.
4. The model stays static on the server and answers incoming user requests.

Plaintext
  [ Raw Data Collection ] ──> [ Train Model Offline ] ──> [ Test Model ] ──> [ Deploy to Server ]
3. The Lifecycle & Workflow of Batch Learning
Data Gathering: Collect historical data (e.g., streaming history for a recommendation engine).

Offline Training: Train the model on your machine or a cloud server using the full dataset.

Testing: Verify if the model's predictions are accurate.

Deployment: Push the model to the production server.

Serving Users: The model handles user queries (e.g., suggesting movies on Netflix).

4. Limitations and Disadvantages of Batch Learning
While batch learning is widely used, it has major drawbacks in certain scenarios:

A. The Business Scenario Changes (Stale Models)
Problem: Real-world trends evolve constantly. If you build a recommendation system today, it knows today's movies. But next week, new movies will release, and user preferences will shift. If your model doesn't update, it becomes outdated (stale).

Solution in Practice: Companies schedule regular retraining cycles (e.g., retraining the model every 24 hours, once a week, or once a month) by combining old data with new data, testing it, and redeploying it.

B. Massive Data Scaling Issues
As companies grow (e.g., social media platforms), data multiplies rapidly every month. If your dataset becomes too massive, your local machines or tools might crash trying to process the entire batch at once.

C. Hardware & Connectivity Limitations (Edge Cases)
If your ML model runs on a device with no internet connectivity (e.g., an app used by soldiers in remote areas like Ladakh, or software inside a satellite/train), you cannot easily pull the model back, retrain it on new data, and redeploy it.

D. Sudden Trend Shifts (Real-time News)
Imagine a social media app where a sudden breaking news event happens (e.g., demonetization or a major global event). Millions of users instantly shift their interests to this topic.

If you are using batch learning scheduled to update only once every 24 hours, your model will fail to catch real-time trends immediately, serving old or irrelevant content to users for a whole day.
